of australia  rather than victoria the australian open  have looked at his visa and looked at  the supporting evidence with that around  the medical exemption and have decided  that that is not sufficient to to gain  entry to australia and they have  contacted the state government last  night and asked whether the state  government would do anything further to  support  novak djokovic's entry  into australia  the state government has said no we  can't do anything further to support it  this is how it is and therefore the  australian border force has made the  decision to cancel his visa and is  aiming to deport him today back to  serbia but there is more to play out  potentially in the courts  i mean there's been a lot of negative  reaction and a lot of strong feelings in  australia since well since we talked 24  hours ago  what are people saying now what's the  reaction now to this latest news  they're pretty much flabbergasted um you  know everyone who didn't want him to  come anyway is just saying well that's  great you know turn him around get him  out of here but i think overall the  sense is just that um how could it come  to this how could it be  that the two processes that should be  far more aligned and far smoother i mean  you've got a situation where you have  state governments here in australia and  i know this is the case in some other  countries around the world but state  governments who are responsible for a b  and c  and then federal governments responsible  for d e and f and the two  just not meeting not not talking not  understanding the process but you know  there's that and so how did it come to  that but also from novak djokovic's  position um and his team how could it be  that that these visa requirements were  not ticked off  so robustly before he took that  photograph and announced that he was on  his way to australia i mean everyone  knows that getting into a country with  the right visa requirements is  absolutely the first thing you have 